#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;

use Game::Cribbage::Terminal;

our $VERSION = "0.18";

exit Game::Cribbage::Terminal->run(@ARGV);

__END__

=head1 NAME

cribbage - cribbage on a terminal

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    cribbage              # arrow keys if the terminal has them
    cribbage --no-pick    # type the card numbers instead

=head1 DESCRIPTION

A front end and nothing else: the screen lives in L<Game::Cribbage::Terminal>
and the rules in L<Game::Cribbage::Board>.

C<--pick> chooses cards with the arrow keys and C<--no-pick> types them. Left
alone it picks when there is a terminal and L<Term::ReadKey> is installed and
types when there is not, so a game played down a pipe needs no option at all.

B<The terminal is put back the way it was> on the way out, from C<INT>, from
C<TERM> and after a die, because a program that leaves a terminal in cbreak
leaves the shell unusable.

=head1 EXIT STATUS

    0   you won, or you quit
    1   you lost
    2   a bad option

=head1 SEE ALSO

L<Game::Cribbage::Terminal>, L<Game::Cribbage>

=cut
